## Simple Thread-safe Queue Implementation

As the title suggests, this is merely a simple implementation of a thread-safe implementation of a queue. Written in C++, it leverages std::condition_variable from <condition_variable>.

### Inner Workings
The aformentioned thread-safe queue is a class template we've aplty named `threadsafe_queue`:


```cpp
    <template typename T>
        class threadsafe_queue {
            public:
                //...Member functions
            private:
                std::mutex m_mutex;
                std::queue<T> m_data_queue;
                std::condition_variable m_data_condition;

        };
```


You can find the complete class definition inside `include/threadsafe_queue.hxx`.


Instead of `pushing` and `popping` directly into and off `m_data_queue` respectively, our custom member functions first have the threads executing them aquire locks on `m_mutex` before reading or modifying `m_data_queue` to avoid data races.


1. The producer thread pushes some data `T` into `m_data_queue` by first acquiring a lock on said queue. Next, it calls `m_data_condition.notify_one()`.
2. A sleeping `consumer` thread will be woken up, lock `m_mutex`, and check if there's indeed some data awaiting consumption inside `m_data_queue`. If there is not, it goes back to sleep, and releases the lock. It checks because in the case of multiple consumer threads, another thread might have woken up earlier after `m_data_condition.notify_one()` and already processed the data.